<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Niassa Company</h1><p class="st-meta">1891 CE – 1929 CE · capital Porto Amelia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Portuguese chartered company that governed northern Mozambique for almost four decades, chiefly as a source of labour and taxes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lacking the money to occupy northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Mozambique</a>, Portugal granted a charter in 1891 to the Companhia do Niassa, covering the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruvuma-river">Rovuma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurio-river">Lúrio</a> rivers from the coast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>. Much of its capital came from British and South African investors, and its headquarters moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibo-island">Ibo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pemba-mozambique">Porto Amélia</a>, now Pemba.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The company had sovereign powers to tax, build ports and grant land, but invested little. Its revenue came mainly from hut tax and from recruiting workers for South African mines. It conquered the interior only slowly, defeating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mataka-chiefdom">Mataka Chiefdom</a> of the Yao in 1912. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> German forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> invaded its territory.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When its charter expired in 1929, Portugal did not renew it, and the region came under direct administration as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>. The company left a reputation for neglect and forced labour.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/niassa-company.jpg" alt="Niassa Company" width="320" height="406"><figcaption>Image: Nyassa Company / Waterlow and Sons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nyassa_1901_stamp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1891 CE – 1929 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Porto Amelia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Emakhuwa, Chiyao, Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Companhia do Niassa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1910&amp;focus=niassa-company" aria-label="Niassa Company on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5713 2710 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Niassa Company lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 3196h0M5861 3386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6033 2966h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6033 2966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mozambique. <a href="/atlas?year=1910&amp;focus=niassa-company">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

